Z||000000||XDrewX||08-05-2001||10:27 PM||||I searched the site but could not find an answer to the following question........    <BR>I am purchasing 17" x 7" rims from tire rack (supposed to fit Fiero)...i would like to put 215/45/17 on front and 235/45/17 on rear.<BR>Anybody else have this combination????||||reg||1
Z||000001||sluppy123||08-05-2001||10:50 PM||||I'm running that set-up on my 87 GT. I'm running Kumho ECSTA Supra 712 tires on 17x7 Mille Miglia wheels.<P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>87' GT-Galaxy silver mettalic, Turbo Charged, Modified 2.8, Eibach Sport springs, RSM side scoops, Mr Mikes leather, 17" Mille Miglia's. <BR>                <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1711832&a=12781563&p=48213929&Sequence=0">   <BR><p>[This message has been edited by sluppy123 (edited 08-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||08-05-2001||11:13 PM||||For any 7" wheel, the widest series you would want to go is 235's. Anything wider and the tire won't be supported properly and your handling will suffer.<BR>The set-up you listed (215's in front and 235's in back) sounds good. Is your car lowered? If not you will have a large gap between wheel wells and tires. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||XDrewX||08-05-2001||11:56 PM||||hey Sluppy123.....I believe it or not, i am buying the exact same tires and rims you have......i havent lowered my car YET.....what method/parts did you use to lower your car......it looks perfect....how are your tires in the rain?<P>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||sluppy123||08-06-2001||06:52 PM||||Rain???????? My car very rarely sees the rain, so I don't know how they handle when wet. But they have GREAT dry traction. It's lowered with Eibach Sport springs.||||reg||1
Z||000005||WaltR||08-06-2001||08:26 PM||||I am running 215/45-17 front and 235/45-17 rear. However I have 17 X 8 Konig Vilians, look similar bot have narrower spokes. Check with the tire manufacturers, 235s are too wide for a 7 inch rim. Should be at least 7.5 wide. I used 8 inch front and rear so i could put 235 front and 245/255 on rear if i swap a 3800sc but have since decided 215 is enough for the front. 215/45 has a lot more tread than the stock 215/6- 14s did. I am running Yokohama A520 tires. These are exelent in the rain and also work well dry.Car is lowered 1 3/4 inches by cutting the coils. There are no problems with the bump stops ore ride even with KYB gas shocks and struts.||||reg||1
Z||000006||jdbaker||08-06-2001||10:59 PM||||Brian:<P>I love the look of your rims, but I can't seem to find that exact style at Tire Rack.<P>What version of Mille Migila's are they?<P>Thanks!<P>J.D.||||reg||1
Z||000007||montage||08-08-2001||08:33 AM||||The width of the wheel necessary is dependent not only on the tire width but the profile or series. All TIrerack tires list on the tire specification page the range of wheel widths that fit and the wheel width used to measure tire dimensions. Due to the large weight of most aftermarket wheels (except racing wheels Racing Hart, SSR, etc) I choose to stay stock. I found Dunlop 8000's in 225/50/15 front and 245/50/15 rear the exact widths I wanted and these tires are measured at the factory using the stock wheel widths of 6 and 7". I must say I am very pleased so far.||||reg||1
Z||000008||montage||08-08-2001||08:37 AM||||The width of the wheel necessary is dependent not only on the tire width but the profile series (smaller the profile the wider the wheel). All TIrerack tires list on the tire specification page the range of wheel widths that fit and the wheel width used to measure tire dimensions. Due to the large weight of most aftermarket wheels (except racing wheels Racing Hart, SSR, etc) I choose to stay stock. I found Dunlop 8000's in 225/50/15 front and 245/50/15 rear the exact widths I wanted and these tires a measured at the factory using the stock wheel widths of 6 and 7". I must say I am very pleased so far.||||reg||1
Z||000009||artherd||08-09-2001||02:07 AM||||215/45/17 seems very narrow for an 8" wheel....<P>I have 215/45/17 on the front of my car, using 7" wheels, and would not go any narrower.<P>Also would not go wider than 235/40/17 (245-255 need 8" rims IMO) <P>The tyres may mount, and may not even blow out, but the sidewalls will be streatched.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000010||WaltR||08-09-2001||03:17 PM||||Yokohama recommends 7.0-8.0 for a 215/45-17 7.5-9.0 for a 235/45-17 or 245/45-17 and 8.0-9.5 for a 255/45-17. I only used an 8.0 because I was going to go to a 235 in front. They would be best on a 7.5 wide wheel. I have since decided it is not necessary to go wider in front. The 215 has an 8 inch tread width instead of the 6.5 inch for the 215/60-14 tires.||||reg||1
